  data: {
    LLM: "Game: Coup d'Etat. Six hand types per round; Director leads; follow suit; penalties to Director; Coup and Hidden Coup for Directorship; winner is most money.",
    Player: "Coup d'Etat: six hands per round (Barbu, Waterloo, Siege, Touche, Guillotine, Dominos). Director chooses hand; follow suit when able; highest in suit wins trick. Coup card holder may attempt Coup to become Director. Most money wins.",
    objective: 'Accumulate the most money by the end of the game. Each round has six hands; the Director chooses which hand type to play. In Director play, players pay penalties to the Director for taking forbidden cards or tricks. The Coup card holder may attempt a Coup (or Hidden Coup in Waterloo, Siege, Guillotine) to overthrow the Director and collect from others.',
    gameplay: 'After the deal, the Coup card holder announces whether they will try for a Coup. If not, they discard the Coup card and take the center card. The Director then selects one of the six hands (Barbu, Waterloo, Siege, Touche, Guillotine, Dominos) and leads. In trick-taking hands, players must follow suit if possible; highest card in the suit led wins the trick. Penalties are paid to the Director as each hand specifies. In Dominos, play is by sequences (next higher or lower in suit or same rank in another suit); first out of cards wins the hand. A successful Coup pays the attemptor from each player and makes them Director; a failed Coup pays into the Treasury. Rounds equal the number of players; left of original Director starts the next round.',
    keyRules: [
      'Six hands per round: Barbu (avoid King of Hearts), Waterloo (avoid Queens), Siege (avoid Spades), Touche (avoid first and last tricks), Guillotine (avoid all of the above), Dominos (be first out of cards).',
      'Director chooses which hand to play each deal; Coup card holder may try to overthrow or pass.',
      'Must follow suit when able; highest in suit led wins the trick except in Dominos.',
      'Penalties in francs paid to Director; failed Coup pays Treasury; successful Coup collects from each player and becomes Director.',
    ],
    bonusRuleGuids: [],
    useTrump: false,
    trumpBonusValues: null,
    setup: {
      players: 'Two to six players; each plays for himself; partnerships not permitted. For three players, remove sevens and eights from the deck.',
      deck: 'Standard 32-card pack (eight cards per suit: Ace, 10, King, Queen, Jack, 9, 8, 7) plus one special Coup card. Suits are Spades, Hearts, Diamonds, Clubs; Ace is highest, then 10, K, Q, J, 9, 8, 7.',
      equipment: '32-card deck (or standard 52 with agreed stripping), optional Coup card or designated wild card; play money in francs (e.g. 10,000 per player).',
      dealing: 'Director shuffles and deals eight cards face down to each player and places one card face down in the center. If the center card is the Coup card, Director shuffles and deals again.',
    },
    turnFlow: 'After the deal, the Coup card holder announces whether they will try for a Coup. If not, they discard the Coup card and take the center card. The Director then selects one of the six hands (Barbu, Waterloo, Siege, Touche, Guillotine, Dominos) and leads. In trick-taking hands, players must follow suit if possible; highest card in the suit led wins the trick. Penalties are paid to the Director as each hand specifies. In Dominos, play is by sequences (next higher or lower in suit or same rank in another suit); first out of cards wins the hand. A successful Coup pays the attemptor from each player and makes them Director; a failed Coup pays into the Treasury. Rounds equal the number of players; left of original Director starts the next round.',
